WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:11.316
This video shows how to put an animation from Pixodesk 2D Animator on a plain web page, as a Lottie file. It takes one script and one element.

2
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.832
Let's start with the page as it is.

3
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:25.419
A heading, and nothing that moves yet. The lottie web player is already in the folder, next to the page.

4
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:32.962
Now for the animation itself. Let's switch to the editor, where a football is already drawn.

5
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.086
It's a vector drawing, standing still.

6
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.998
To make it move, let's switch on Auto mode.

7
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.948
With Auto mode on, every change becomes a keyframe.

8
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.354
Half a second in, let's drag the ball down to the floor.

9
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.236
A keyframe appears on the timeline, holding the new position.

10
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:01.217
At one second, let's bring it back up.

11
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:07.858
Now it falls and rises again, in one second.

12
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:15.996
A real ball speeds up as it falls, and slows down as it rises. Let's set that easing.

13
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:27.313
Gravity easing, falling and rising, makes the bounce feel natural.

14
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:34.019
The animation is ready. Let's save it as Lottie JSON, next to the page.

15
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:41.413
Now, back to our page.

16
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.434
The animation file is already there, next to the page.

17
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.951
Let's load the player, and start the animation, at the end of the page.

18
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:12.090
Load animation takes the container, the file's path, and loop and autoplay.

19
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:16.024
Then, let's add the element it plays in.

20
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.796
The container is this element, found by its id.

21
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.698
Let's reload the page, and watch.

22
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:40.013
There it is. The ball bounces, on a plain web page.

23
00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:50.628
So, one script, one Lottie file saved from the editor, and one element. That's all it takes to bring an animation onto a web page.
